<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Nawanagar State</h1><p class="st-meta">1540 CE – 1948 CE (approx.) · capital Jamnagar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Jadeja Rajput state of Saurashtra ruled by the Jams of Jamnagar, home of the cricketer Ranjitsinhji and a wartime refuge for Polish children.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1540 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jam-rawal">Jam Rawal</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jadeja">Jadeja Rajput</a> from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/cutch-state">Kutch</a>, conquered the Halar region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathiawar">Saurashtra</a> and founded Nawanagar, the 'new town', later called Jamnagar after the dynasty's title.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Nawanagar was known for its pearl fisheries, textiles and port. In 1591 its forces fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bhuchar-mori">Bhuchar Mori</a>, after which the Jams accepted imperial rule. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjitsinhji">Jam Ranjitsinhji</a> (r. 1907–1933), the famous cricketer, modernised Jamnagar. His nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/digvijaysinhji">Digvijaysinhji</a> sheltered hundreds of Polish refugee children at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balachadi">Balachadi</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1948 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/digvijaysinhji">Digvijaysinhji</a> became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajpramukh">Rajpramukh</a> of the new <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/saurashtra-state">United State of Saurashtra</a>, into which Nawanagar merged. Jamnagar is now a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/nawanagar-state.jpg" alt="Nawanagar State" width="320" height="262"><figcaption>Image: Hunter, William Wilson, Sir, (1840-1900), Cotton, James Sutherland, (1847-1918) ed. Burn, Richard, Sir, (1871-1947) j... · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bombay_Prov_north_1909.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1540 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jamnagar</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Jam Rawal, Ranjitsinhji, Digvijaysinhji</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gujarati</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>India</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Navanagar, Jamnagar, Halar</dd></dl></aside></div>
